\title{Formal Limits of Molecular Computing: Discrete Reaction Networks,
Parallelism, and Storage}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Molecular computing proposes to perform computation with populations of molecules
that transform one another through chemical reactions. We develop a rigorous
discrete framework for such systems and prove a coherent body of results
delimiting their power. We adopt the standard \emph{population} (Petri-net)
semantics of a chemical reaction network (CRN): states are molecule-count vectors
and reactions consume reactant complexes and create product complexes. We prove
that this dynamics is \emph{strongly monotone} and \emph{translation-invariant},
that \emph{coverability} is upward monotone, and that any balanced linear
functional is a \emph{conserved quantity} along every trajectory. From
monotonicity we derive the central structural limitation: \emph{no reaction can
detect the absence of a species} (no zero-test), which is precisely the
obstruction that keeps the exact discrete model strictly below Turing power.
Turning to performance, we formalize the folklore that molecular parallelism gives
only a \emph{constant-factor speedup}: if work $W$ is spread across $T$ steps with
at most $p$ operations per step, then $W \le T \cdot p$, whence the speedup is at
most $p$; and with a fixed volume cap $P$ on simultaneous operations, exponential
work forces unbounded parallel time, $T \ge 2^n/P$. Finally, we establish
information-theoretic \emph{storage bounds}: a register of $k$ two-state units
distinguishes at most $2^k$ inputs, so distinguishing $N$ inputs requires
$k \ge \log_2 N$ units, and any injective description scheme for $M$ behaviors
requires $k \ge \log_2 M$ --- a discrete shadow of the ``volume proportional to
Kolmogorov complexity'' principle. All results are elementary in statement, exact
where possible, and proved from first principles.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{molecular computer} is a physical system --- canonically a solution of DNA
strands or other reactive species --- that carries out computation through
chemical reactions rather than through electronic logic gates. The appeal is
twofold. First, the information density of matter is extraordinary: a cubic
micrometer can hold an enormous number of molecules, each a potential unit of
state. Second, all those molecules react \emph{in parallel}, suggesting a natural,
massively concurrent substrate for computation. These observations have fueled
ambitious conjectures: that a speck of DNA might store on the order of $10^{18}$
bits, that molecular parallelism might crush NP-complete problems, and that
chemical reaction networks might be universal computers.

Each of these claims is partly true and partly mistaken, and the boundary between
the two is sharp and mathematical. This paper draws that boundary. We work in the
discrete, population-level model of chemical reaction networks, which abstracts a
reacting mixture as a bag of molecules undergoing state transitions. Within this
model we prove: structural laws of the dynamics (strong monotonicity, translation
invariance, monotone coverability, conservation of balanced functionals); a
universality obstruction (no absence detection); a parallelism bound (constant,
volume-capped speedup); and information-theoretic storage and description bounds.

\section{The discrete model of a chemical reaction network}

Throughout, let $S$ be a type of \emph{species}. A \emph{state} is a function
$x : S \to \N$ recording the count $x(s)$ of each species. States are ordered
pointwise: $x \le y$ means $x(s) \le y(s)$ for all $s$. Addition and truncated
subtraction are pointwise, with $\N$-subtraction $a - b = \max(a-b,0)$.

\begin{definition}[Reaction]
A \emph{reaction} $r$ consists of two states, the \emph{reactant complex}
$r.\reactant : S \to \N$ and the \emph{product complex} $r.\product : S \to \N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Enabled]
A reaction $r$ is \emph{enabled} at $x$, written $r.\enabled(x)$, when
$r.\reactant \le x$: every reactant molecule required is present.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Firing]
Firing $r$ at $x$ yields the state
$r.\fire(x)(s) = x(s) - r.\reactant(s) + r.\product(s)$.
On enabled states the truncated subtraction is exact.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[CRN, step, reachability]
A \emph{chemical reaction network} is a finite list $rs$ of reactions. A
\emph{step} relates $x$ to $y$, written $\Step_{rs}(x,y)$, when some $r \in rs$ is
enabled at $x$ and $y = r.\fire(x)$. \emph{Reachability} $\Reach_{rs}$ is the
reflexive--transitive closure of $\Step_{rs}$.
\end{definition}

This is exactly the vector-addition-system / Petri-net view of mass-action
kinetics, stripped to its combinatorial core.

\section{Structural laws of the dynamics}

\subsection{Monotonicity}

\begin{lemma}[Enabledness is upward closed]
If $r$ is enabled at $x$ and $x \le y$, then $r$ is enabled at $y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$r.\reactant \le x \le y$ by transitivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strong monotonicity]
If $r$ is enabled at $x$, then $r.\fire(x + d) = r.\fire(x) + d$ for every surplus
$d : S \to \N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix a species $s$. Since $r.\reactant(s) \le x(s)$, the truncated subtraction is
exact, so
$(x(s) + d(s)) - r.\reactant(s) + r.\product(s)
= \big(x(s) - r.\reactant(s) + r.\product(s)\big) + d(s)$
over $\N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monotone firing]
If $r$ is enabled at $x$ and $x \le y$, then $r.\fire(x) \le r.\fire(y)$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Translation invariance}

\begin{theorem}[Step shift]
If $\Step_{rs}(x,y)$ then $\Step_{rs}(x+d, y+d)$ for every $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $r$ witness the step. Then $r$ is enabled at $x + d$, and
$r.\fire(x+d) = r.\fire(x) + d = y + d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reachability shift]
If $\Reach_{rs}(x,y)$ then $\Reach_{rs}(x+d, y+d)$ for every $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on the reflexive--transitive closure, applying the previous theorem.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Coverability}

\begin{definition}[Coverable]
A target $t$ is \emph{coverable} from $x$ if $\exists y,\ \Reach_{rs}(x,y) \wedge
t \le y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Coverability is upward monotone]
If $x \le x'$ and $t$ is coverable from $x$, then $t$ is coverable from $x'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $y$ witness coverability from $x$. Put $d = x' - x$, so $x + d = x'$. By the
reachability shift, $\Reach_{rs}(x', y+d)$, and $t \le y \le y + d$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Conservation laws}

Let $S$ be finite and $w : S \to \Z$ a weighting. Its value on a state is
$\massfn_w(x) = \sum_{s \in S} w(s)\, x(s)$.

\begin{definition}[Conserved by a reaction]
$w$ is \emph{conserved} by $r$ if $\sum_s w(s)\, r.\product(s) = \sum_s w(s)\,
r.\reactant(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance under firing]
If $w$ is conserved by $r$ and $r$ is enabled at $x$, then
$\massfn_w(r.\fire(x)) = \massfn_w(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On enabled states $r.\fire(x)(s) = x(s) - r.\reactant(s) + r.\product(s)$ over
$\Z$. Substituting and splitting the sum, the reactant and product terms cancel by
the balance condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conservation along trajectories]
If every reaction of $rs$ conserves $w$, then $\massfn_w(y) = \massfn_w(x)$
whenever $\Reach_{rs}(x,y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on reachability using the previous theorem.
\end{proof}

\section{The fundamental limitation: no zero-test}

\begin{theorem}[No zero-test / no absence detector]
Let $S$ have decidable equality and fix a species $s_0$. There is no reaction $r$
whose enabling condition coincides with ``$s_0$ is absent'':
\[
\neg\; \big(\forall x,\ r.\enabled(x) \iff x(s_0) = 0\big).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose such an $r$ exists. Since $r$ is enabled at its own reactant complex, the
hypothesis forces $r.\reactant(s_0) = 0$. Define $z(s_0) = 1$ and
$z(s) = r.\reactant(s)$ for $s \ne s_0$. Then $r.\reactant \le z$, so
$r.\enabled(z)$; the hypothesis yields $z(s_0) = 0$, contradicting $z(s_0) = 1$.
\end{proof}

The proof exploits monotonicity: enabling is upward closed, but ``$x(s_0) = 0$'' is
downward closed. Consequently the exact discrete mass-action model is only as
strong as a vector addition system / Petri net.

\begin{remark}[Recovering universality]
The obstruction is specific to the exact, deterministic reading. Stochastic CRNs
with mass-action rates can simulate register machines with unbounded time and
vanishing error probability (Soloveichik--Cook--Winfree--Bruck); universality
reappears once one adds a probabilistic, continuous-time layer and tolerates error.
\end{remark}

\section{Molecular parallelism yields only a constant-factor speedup}

We abstract a computation by its \emph{work} $W$ and a \emph{schedule} over $T$
steps, where $\ops(t)$ operations occur at step $t$, each bounded by the
parallelism $p$.

\begin{theorem}[Work--time bound]
If $\ops(t) \le p$ for all $t \in \{0,\dots,T-1\}$ and $W \le \sum_t \ops(t)$, then
$W \le T \cdot p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$W \le \sum_t \ops(t) \le \sum_t p = T \cdot p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parallel time lower bound]
Under the same hypotheses with $p \ge 1$, $\lfloor W / p \rfloor \le T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Divide $W \le T \cdot p$ by $p$ and cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Speedup at most $p$]
Under the same hypotheses, $W \le p \cdot T$; the speedup $W/T \le p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Commute the product in the work--time bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Volume-bounded speedup]
If the device admits at most $P$ simultaneously active molecules ($p \le P$), then
$W \le P \cdot T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Chain $W \le p \cdot T \le P \cdot T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No exponential speedup]
Suppose a family of problems has work at least $2^n$, run on a device of fixed
volume so that $2^n \le P \cdot T_{\mathrm{par}}(n)$ for all $n$. Then
$T_{\mathrm{par}}$ is unbounded: no constant $C$ bounds it.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $T_{\mathrm{par}}(n) \le C$ for all $n$, then $2^n \le P \cdot C$ for all $n$,
contradicting the unboundedness of $2^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative exponential lower bound]
With $P \ge 1$ and $2^n \le P \cdot T_{\mathrm{par}}(n)$ for all $n$,
$T_{\mathrm{par}}(n) \ge \lfloor 2^n / P \rfloor$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Divide the hypothesis by $P$ and cancel.
\end{proof}

\section{Information-theoretic storage and description bounds}

Model $k$ bits of molecular state as functions $\mathrm{Fin}\,k \to \mathrm{Bool}$,
of which there are exactly $2^k$.

\begin{theorem}[Storage capacity]
If $I$ is finite and $\mathrm{config} : I \to (\mathrm{Fin}\,k \to \mathrm{Bool})$
is injective, then $|I| \le 2^k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An injection into a set of size $2^k$ bounds the domain cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bit lower bound]
Under the same hypotheses, $\log_2 |I| \le k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the monotone $\log_2$ to $|I| \le 2^k$ and simplify $\log_2 2^k = k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Insufficient volume]
If $2^k < |I|$, no injective encoding $I \to (\mathrm{Fin}\,k \to \mathrm{Bool})$
exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the storage capacity bound by contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Kolmogorov-style volume lower bound]
For a finite family $B$ of distinct behaviors, any injective description scheme
$\mathrm{descr} : B \to (\mathrm{Fin}\,k \to \mathrm{Bool})$ satisfies
$\log_2 |B| \le k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Specialize the bit lower bound to $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density sanity check]
$2^{59} < 10^{18} \le 2^{60}$. Hence a $59$-unit register holds fewer than
$10^{18}$ configurations, while $60$ units suffice.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct numerical verification.
\end{proof}

Since the number of two-state molecules --- and hence the volume --- scales with
$k$, these bounds are the discrete shadow of the principle that minimum volume is
bounded below by descriptive (Kolmogorov) complexity.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

On the side of \emph{power}, the discrete model is provably limited: monotonicity
forbids absence detection, and volume-bounded parallelism forbids exponential
speedup. On the side of \emph{structure}, monotonicity delivers translation
invariance and monotone coverability, while balance delivers conservation laws. On
the side of \emph{resources}, information theory pins storage at $\log_2 N$ units
and volume at the log of the behavioral repertoire. A recurring theme is that the
model's greatest strength and its sharpest weakness are the same property.

Natural next steps: (1) formalize stochastic CRNs simulating register machines
with vanishing error to recover Turing power; (2) prove decidability of
coverability via well-quasi-order machinery; (3) derive Farkas-style
non-reachability certificates from conservation laws.

\section{Conclusion}

Molecular computing is neither omnipotent nor illusory. In the exact discrete
model, chemical reaction networks are monotone, conservative, blind to absence,
capped in speedup by their volume, and bounded in memory by information. These are
theorems, and they point ingenuity where it can succeed.

